  "cause": "**The largest Ebola outbreak in the Democratic Republic of the Congo's history is outpacing the response in the one province where the response cannot reach.** As of **11 November**, the DRC had reported **11,114 confirmed cases** and **5,433 deaths** from Ebola disease caused by **Bundibugyo virus**, according to the **ECDC** — a case fatality rate of roughly **49 per cent** — across **eight provinces**: Ituri, Nord-Kivu, Sud-Kivu, Tshopo, Haut-Uélé, Bas-Uélé, Sud-Ubangi and Nord-Ubangi. The **WHO**'s latest disease outbreak notice counted **1,463 new confirmed cases in the 21 days to 3 November** — about **70 a day** — with **Ituri** accounting for **710** of them and **Nord-Kivu 640**; Ituri alone has recorded **7,485 cases and 3,532 deaths**, with cases in **28 of its 36 health zones**. The outbreak began in **May 2026** and was declared a **public health emergency of international concern**. It has crossed borders: **Kenya** reported **nine** cases including **two deaths**, all linked to a single importation, and has now completed 21 days of contact quarantine with seven recoveries; **Nigeria** reported one case, medically evacuated to **Switzerland**, with **62** contacts traced; **Uganda**'s outbreak has ended. Four cases linked to the DRC outbreak have been evacuated or imported into the **EU/EEA** — two to Germany, one to the Netherlands, one to France — plus a precautionary evacuation to the UK, every one of them a health or humanitarian worker. **The new epicentre is defined by violence rather than by epidemiology.** The **New York Times** reports from the outbreak's new centre that it is *\"a region scarred by rebel violence\"* and *\"largely isolated from aid by an armed group that has killed and kidnapped aid workers\"* — its headline sets the two threats side by side: *\"Caught Between Killers: Ebola on One Side, Islamic State on the Other.\"* Health workers at the epicentre are reported to be working with little pay and little rest; treatment centres have been attacked, and residents burned one in May as anger over the outbreak grew. The virus is **Bundibugyo**, not Zaire ebolavirus — the species against which the stockpiled vaccines and antibody treatments were developed — and the case fatality now recorded is far above the roughly 17 per cent reported in the outbreak's early weeks. **Kenya** is counting down to the end of its own outbreak; **Congo** is counting cases it cannot reach.",
  "bullets": [
   "**Where the road is closed, the epidemiology is written by the armed group** — a new epicentre in a rebel-scarred region, aid workers killed and kidnapped, responders unable to enter health zones already reporting cases; the direct effect is that a fraction of transmission is observed and a smaller fraction interrupted; the compound consequence is that the outbreak's true reproduction number is set by the parts of Ituri nobody is counting, so every national and international projection is an underestimate — and policy built on an underestimate is always one response cycle behind the virus",
   "**A 49 per cent case fatality rate is a statement about access, not about virulence** — 5,433 deaths among 11,114 confirmed cases, against about 17 per cent early in the outbreak; the direct effect is that people arrive at treatment late or not at all; the compound consequence is that each untreated death is also an unsafe burial, which is the highest-transmission event in an Ebola outbreak — so the mortality rate and the infection rate reinforce each other, and both are governed by the same closed road",
   "**Bundibugyo is the species the stockpile was not built for** — vaccines and therapeutics licensed and pre-positioned against Zaire ebolavirus do not straightforwardly cover a different species, and the trials that would answer the question need the access the response does not have; the direct effect is that the classic ring-vaccination playbook cannot simply be executed; the compound consequence is that the outbreak becomes a test case for how the world handles a haemorrhagic fever whose countermeasures are experimental — and the answer will be produced in Ituri, under fire, or not at all",
   "**Health workers are the system, and they are being killed, kidnapped and left unpaid** — aid workers killed and kidnapped by the armed group, staff at the epicentre working with little pay or rest, a treatment centre burned by residents in May; the direct effect is that each attack removes capacity that cannot be replaced quickly, because Ebola responders are trained and scarce; the compound consequence is that the outbreak selects for the collapse of trust — communities that have seen a centre burned and a worker kidnapped do not report symptoms, and an outbreak that is not reported is an outbreak that is not traced",
   "**Four evacuations to Europe are the outbreak's early-warning system, and they are all healthcare workers** — Germany twice, the Netherlands, France, plus a UK precautionary evacuation, in an outbreak that has already reached Kenya and Nigeria; the direct effect is that European hospital preparedness is now live rather than theoretical; the compound consequence is that the political response to the next imported case will be shaped by border policy rather than by epidemiology — and a travel ban on the region, the most likely political reaction, would close the last air bridge the response depends on"
  ],
  "verse": "Provide for (feed and clothe) those who give you signs (evidence) of their poverty (need) so that things may be fair to them and you, and so that no powerful rain of disgust (inhumanity) may sweep down over you which will drown you in shame and guilt, such that you find a miserable end in the estimation of people of your kind (fellow human beings).",
  "verseSrc": "Chapter 7, Verse 85",
  "hinge": "§7:85 does not ask for sympathy; it asks for provision, and it attaches a consequence to refusal that is social rather than supernatural — *shame and guilt* in the estimation of others. The provision Ituri needs is not primarily money. It is access: a negotiated, escorted humanitarian corridor to the new epicentre, agreed with the armed group's commanders through whatever intermediary they will talk to, with a published convoy schedule and a notification mechanism so that a vehicle carrying responders is not a target. That is a hard negotiation, and it is the whole task — because every other instrument depends on it: the ring-vaccination trial, the safe-burial teams, the contact tracers, the laboratory samples. Alongside it, two things that need no permission. Publish the weekly health-zone table for Ituri in full, so the gap between reported and estimated cases is visible and cannot be managed by omission. And pay the responders — hazard pay, on time, funded through the WHO and Africa CDC rather than through a provincial budget that has already failed to pay them once. §7:86 adds the frame: *do not foment chaos on Earth… keep your world in all things in regulation so that you do not destroy it.* A virus is not chaos of our making, but the road that keeps the responders out is."
